> On Mon, 2025-03-17 at 16:00 +0000, Finn Lindgren wrote:
> > It depends on what aspect of the model performance you're interested in. CPO
> > does "what it says on the tin", and one just has to interpret the values
> > accordingly; when the possibly discrete value outcomes number in the
> > thousands, and there is inherent randomness in the observation stage, then one
> > simply can't expect large prediction probabilities. One option is to use other
> > proper scores such as squared error or absolute error. What is useful depends
> > entirely on your specific problem. In other words, think about what _you_ care
> > about, and find a metric that evaluates that. This also involved choosing
> > something other than "leave-one-out" prediction, but e.g. using group-cv
> > doesn't remove the issue of what metric to evaluate for such group-leave-out
> > predictions.
> >
> > Finn
> >
> > On Mon, 17 Mar 2025 at 15:27, Bram <BramPar...@live.nl> wrote:
> > > Hi Finn,
> > > Thanks for the example, that makes it very clear what CPO is/does. What
> > > would you use instead to assess the model performance (without comparing it
> > > to other models)?
> > > Bram
> > >
> > > Op maandag 17 maart 2025 om 15:20:22 UTC+1 schreef Finn Lindgren:
> > > > Hi Bram,
> > > >
> > > > since CPO = Pr(Y_i|Y_{not i}) according to the estimated model, then
> > > > values in the (0.00,0.011) can be entirely reasonable for discrete data;
> > > > For example, consider an outcome of 200 when the estimated posterior
> > > > conditional prediction model is Binomial(1000, 0.2), i.e. the observed
> > > > value is equal to the expectation, and thus should be consider a good
> > > > prediction.
> > > > The CPO is then
> > > > > dbinom(200,1000,0.2)
> > > > [1] 0.03152536
> > > >
> > > > You cannot interpret CPO values outside the context of what the model is
> > > > (discrete/continuous, overall size etc), and the main use is to compare
> > > > _different_ models for the same data. Finn
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > On Mon, 17 Mar 2025 at 14:11, Bram <BramPar...@live.nl> wrote:
