> > > > On Mon, 2025-03-17 at 16:00 +0000, Finn Lindgren wrote:
> > > > > It depends on what aspect of the model performance you're interested in.
> > > > > CPO
> > > > > does "what it says on the tin", and one just has to interpret the values
> > > > > accordingly; when the possibly discrete value outcomes number in the
> > > > > thousands, and there is inherent randomness in the observation stage,
> > > > > then one
> > > > > simply can't expect large prediction probabilities. One option is to use
> > > > > other
> > > > > proper scores such as squared error or absolute error. What is useful
> > > > > depends
> > > > > entirely on your specific problem. In other words, think about what
> > > > > _you_ care
> > > > > about, and find a metric that evaluates that. This also involved
> > > > > choosing
> > > > > something other than "leave-one-out" prediction, but e.g. using group-cv
> > > > > doesn't remove the issue of what metric to evaluate for such group-
> > > > > leave-out
> > > > > predictions.
> > > > >
> > > > > Finn
> > > > >
