> > > > > > > Hi Bram,
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > since CPO = Pr(Y_i|Y_{not i}) according to the estimated model, then
> > > > > > > values in the (0.00,0.011) can be entirely reasonable for discrete
> > > > > > > data;
> > > > > > > For example, consider an outcome of 200 when the estimated posterior
> > > > > > > conditional prediction model is Binomial(1000, 0.2), i.e. the
> > > > > > > observed
> > > > > > > value is equal to the expectation, and thus should be consider a
> > > > > > > good
> > > > > > > prediction.
> > > > > > > The CPO is then
> > > > > > > > dbinom(200,1000,0.2)
> > > > > > > [1] 0.03152536
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > You cannot interpret CPO values outside the context of what the
> > > > > > > model is
> > > > > > > (discrete/continuous, overall size etc), and the main use is to
> > > > > > > compare
> > > > > > > _different_ models for the same data. Finn
